---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Work" description="Selected software projects and the decisions behind them.">
  <header class="page-header shell split-header">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">SELECTED WORK</p><h1>Proof lives in<br /><em>the decisions.</em></h1></div>
    <p>Projects are more than screenshots. These are the constraints, tradeoffs, and course corrections that shaped the result.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="case-study shell" id="rough-at-sea">
    <div class="case-study-index">CASE 001</div>
    <div class="case-study-main">
      <div class="case-study-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">PERSONAL PUBLISHING PLATFORM</p><h2>Rough at Sea</h2></div>
        <p class="case-study-year">2026</p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-study-hero">
        <div class="case-radar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>R/S</span></div>
        <div class="case-caption"><span>FROM FULL-STACK</span><strong>TO STATIC-BY-DESIGN</strong></div>
      </div>

      <div class="case-study-story">
        <div><p class="section-number">THE SITUATION</p><h3>A capable backend for a problem that no longer needed one.</h3></div>
        <p>The first version used Next.js, PostgreSQL, Google authentication, server actions, and object storage. It worked. But when the editorial direction changed, keeping that machinery would have meant maintaining complexity without earning value.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Preserve the learning</h3><p>The complete application remains in Git history and under <code>archive/</code>, available as a technical reference without dictating the new structure.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Choose the smaller system</h3><p>Astro generates the site as static HTML. Articles live beside the code in typed Markdown collections, and interactive JavaScript is opt-in.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Make the redesign visible</h3><p>The new interface treats engineering notes as field reports: high-contrast typography, navigational instruments, and a precise editorial grid.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="outcome-strip">
        <div><strong>0</strong><span>databases</span></div>
        <div><strong>0</strong><span>auth flows</span></div>
        <div><strong>1</strong><span>React island</span></div>
        <div><strong>100%</strong><span>static output</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
